WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:03.960
JOE: All individuals transition from
one activity to another and from

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:09.000
one setting to another every day, at
home, school, or in the workplace.

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:12.929
These may involve major
transitions or minor transitions.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:17.229
Individuals with autism often find
it difficult to predict what is

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:20.850
happening in their day as well as
planning and executing transitions.

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:26.400
Transitions whether minor or major for
individuals with autism can be a stressful

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.700
and confusing process that often results
in emotional distress or disengagement.

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:35.379
BETH: Individuals with autism
spectrum disorders may have greater

00:00:35.379 --> 00:00:39.630
difficulty in shifting attention
from one task to another or handling

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:41.500
changes of their routine or setting.

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:45.870
This may be due to a greater need
for predictability, challenges in

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:49.730
understanding what activity will be
coming next, or difficulty when a

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:51.779
pattern of behaviour is disrupted.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.780
JOE: When transition strategies are
used, they can help with reducing

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:59.060
the amount of transition time and
improving appropriate behaviour

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:03.370
during transitions and disruptions
to activities, settings, or routines.

00:01:04.099 --> 00:01:07.939
Strategies can be used before a
transition occurs, during a transition,

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:12.159
and after a transition, and can
be verbal, auditory, or visual.

00:01:12.879 --> 00:01:15.880
They can help to increase
predictability for individuals on

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:19.910
the autism spectrum and to create
positive routines around transitions.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:23.829
BETH: When looking at transitions there
are a number of strategies that can be

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.079
used to support individuals with autism.

00:01:26.759 --> 00:01:30.679
Visual supports such as pictures
or real objects can indicate

00:01:30.699 --> 00:01:34.110
what the transition may involve
and be can be used as a prompt.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:39.500
Visual timers, digital alerts and day
planners can give prior notice and

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.559
warning when transitions may occur.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.779
JOE: Preparing individuals for
transitions in advance though a range

00:01:45.789 --> 00:01:50.050
of different methods can help them
become aware of what to expect and

00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:54.480
making them part of that transition
by involving them in the process can

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.600
help them to feel more in control.

00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:00.779
BETH: Making sure everyone concerned
is aware of the issues, can make things

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:05.359
more consistent in approach and detail,
so that they can reinforce any guidance

00:02:05.389 --> 00:02:07.160
and support that might be needed.

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:13.626
Using these strategies and types
of supports can help prepare

00:02:13.626 --> 00:02:14.940
children and adults to initiate
and carry out a transition and

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:18.930
to demonstrate the appropriate
behaviour relying less on prompting.

